        "prior_assessment": "Lithuania's security posture remains at escalating \u2014 at the highest intensity point in this monitoring cycle. The May 20 Vilnius drone alert represented a qualitative threshold: civilian mass shelter alarm, competence exposure (locked shelters), and Baltic presidential formal demand for NATO to upgrade air policing to full air defense mission with counter-drone capability. Police/border guard corruption in the balloon network added a state-institution penetration dimension. Carl-Gustaf M4 additional order (~\u20ac42M) confirmed the established rearmament pattern. Attribution of drone incidents remains contested.",
        "updated_assessment": "Lithuania's security posture remains at escalating with two new significant capability acquisitions this week that materially advance the division-level buildout. The State Defense Council authorized the purchase of 936 Patria 6x6 APCs from Finland \u2014 the largest single armored vehicle procurement in Lithuanian history, replacing the aging M113 fleet, with Lithuanian industrial participation required and integration into the CAVS multinational program. Simultaneously, the Lithuanian Armed Forces established a new centralized long-range autonomous drone unit (ANBO), institutionalizing what had been distributed drone capabilities developed since 2024. Lithuania's communications regulator confirmed Russia has expanded GPS spoofing capacity from 3 to 36 antennas in Kaliningrad, covering a 450 km radius encompassing the entire Baltic, most of Poland, and parts of Finland and Sweden \u2014 a 12x capacity increase that directly affects civilian and military navigation, confirmed by Reuters at Tier 1. The Seimas defense committee approved a comprehensive crisis management and civil security law reform package \u2014 the direct institutional response to the May 20 shelter access failures \u2014 overhauling shelter assignment, evacuation authority, and alert procedures using Sweden's model. US Project Flytrap counter-drone Stryker live-fire testing at Pabrad\u0117 confirms active bilateral engagement on Lithuania's most pressing capability gap. The German-Dutch corps assignment to take command of NATO land forces in Estonia and Latvia (by mid-2026) deepens regional command architecture, indirectly strengthening the northeastern flank. A single-source WSJ report (via klaipeda.diena.lt) that European intelligence is revising the Russian attack-on-NATO timeline to within 12 months \u2014 if confirmed \u2014 would compress the VSD's 3-5 year assessment significantly and would require a reassessment of procurement urgency.",
