        "prior_assessment": "Lithuania's economic posture remains at routine. Bank of Lithuania enforcement actions (Paysera, Compensa Life fine), housing market data showing rising mortgage values and declining affordability. Defence spending fiscal pressure and IMF 60%-debt-by-2030 warning remain dominant structural watchpoints.",
        "updated_assessment": "Lithuania's economic posture remains at routine with several confirming data points. PM Ruginien\u00e9e publicly ruled out tax increases while forecasting that annual inflation could reach 5% \u2014 acknowledging that Middle East energy disruption (Hormuz-related) is already driving fuel price stress. The government's stated priority is income growth (minimum wage discussions) rather than new levies, with autumn reassessment planned. Budget revenue data (January\u2013April at 30.5% of annual plan, up 8.9% year-on-year in tax receipts) confirms solid near-term fiscal performance, with VAT and personal income tax both running ahead of 2025 pace, driven by domestic consumption and wage growth. The Bank of Lithuania's finding that services exports surpassed goods exports for the first time in 2025 is a structurally interesting economic transformation signal \u2014 consistent with the ICT/fintech/laser technology sector growth documented in the dossier (\u00a76). SEB Baltic merger (ECB approved, Lithuanian operations unifying under Estonian entity by 2027) is routine financial sector consolidation. Finance Minister Vaitiek\u016bnas's call at the NATO PA for larger NATO grants (not just SAFE loans) for eastern flank states explicitly connects fiscal sustainability to the defense spending trajectory \u2014 acknowledging that SAFE loan obligations 'will affect Lithuania's fiscal position and debt growth.' This is the clearest public articulation yet of the defense-fiscal tension the analyst has been tracking as a structural watchpoint. Analysis published by 15min confirming Lithuania's support for Ukraine has not harmed economic growth (GDP grew 2.8% in 2024, 2.9% in 2025 despite aid commitments) addresses a domestic disinformation narrative.",
